WEBVTT

00:00.730 --> 00:02.340
Let's see if we
can give ourselves

00:02.340 --> 00:09.760
an intuitive understanding
of the mean value theorem.

00:09.760 --> 00:14.210
And as we'll see, once you parse
some of the mathematical lingo

00:14.210 --> 00:19.120
and notation, it's actually
a quite intuitive theorem.

00:19.120 --> 00:22.810
And so let's just think
about some function, f.

00:22.810 --> 00:24.910
So let's say I have
some function f.

00:24.910 --> 00:27.330
And we know a few things
about this function.

00:27.330 --> 00:39.100
We know that it is
continuous over the closed

00:39.100 --> 00:44.520
interval between x equals
a and x is equal to b.

00:44.520 --> 00:46.280
And so when we put
these brackets here,

00:46.280 --> 00:48.704
that just means closed interval.

00:48.704 --> 00:50.120
So when I put a
bracket here, that

00:50.120 --> 00:51.840
means we're including
the point a.

00:51.840 --> 00:54.515
And if I put the bracket on
the right hand side instead

00:54.515 --> 00:56.360
of a parentheses,
that means that we

00:56.360 --> 00:57.570
are including the point b.

00:57.570 --> 00:58.986
And continuous
just means we don't

00:58.986 --> 01:01.830
have any gaps or jumps in
the function over this closed

01:01.830 --> 01:03.100
interval.

01:03.100 --> 01:12.210
Now, let's also assume that
it's differentiable over

01:12.210 --> 01:14.810
the open interval
between a and b.

01:14.810 --> 01:16.720
So now we're saying,
well, it's OK

01:16.720 --> 01:18.392
if it's not
differentiable right at a,

01:18.392 --> 01:20.100
or if it's not
differentiable right at b.

01:20.100 --> 01:21.730
And differentiable
just means that there's

01:21.730 --> 01:23.550
a defined derivative,
that you can actually

01:23.550 --> 01:25.810
take the derivative
at those points.

01:25.810 --> 01:30.294
So it's differentiable over the
open interval between a and b.

01:30.294 --> 01:31.710
So those are the
constraints we're

01:31.710 --> 01:33.918
going to put on ourselves
for the mean value theorem.

01:33.918 --> 01:35.940
And so let's just try
to visualize this thing.

01:35.940 --> 01:41.750
So this is my function,
that's the y-axis.

01:41.750 --> 01:46.800
And then this right
over here is the x-axis.

01:46.800 --> 01:49.090
And I'm going to--
let's see, x-axis,

01:49.090 --> 01:51.040
and let me draw my interval.

01:51.040 --> 01:57.730
So that's a, and then
this is b right over here.

01:57.730 --> 02:02.790
And so let's say our function
looks something like this.

02:02.790 --> 02:06.100
Draw an arbitrary
function right over here,

02:06.100 --> 02:09.520
let's say my function
looks something like that.

02:09.520 --> 02:12.915
So at this point right over
here, the x value is a,

02:12.915 --> 02:15.230
and the y value is f(a).

02:17.810 --> 02:21.280
At this point right
over here, the x value

02:21.280 --> 02:24.650
is b, and the y value,
of course, is f(b).

02:30.260 --> 02:32.390
So all the mean
value theorem tells

02:32.390 --> 02:35.880
us is if we take the
average rate of change

02:35.880 --> 02:38.600
over the interval,
that at some point

02:38.600 --> 02:40.870
the instantaneous rate
of change, at least

02:40.870 --> 02:43.140
at some point in
this open interval,

02:43.140 --> 02:44.920
the instantaneous
change is going

02:44.920 --> 02:46.670
to be the same as
the average change.

02:46.670 --> 02:48.530
Now what does that
mean, visually?

02:48.530 --> 02:50.720
So let's calculate
the average change.

02:50.720 --> 02:54.230
The average change between
point a and point b,

02:54.230 --> 02:56.780
well, that's going to be the
slope of the secant line.

03:02.010 --> 03:04.039
So that's-- so this
is the secant line.

03:04.039 --> 03:05.080
So think about its slope.

03:05.080 --> 03:07.500
All the mean value
theorem tells us

03:07.500 --> 03:10.130
is that at some point
in this interval,

03:10.130 --> 03:12.330
the instant slope
of the tangent line

03:12.330 --> 03:14.960
is going to be the same as
the slope of the secant line.

03:14.960 --> 03:17.680
And we can see, just visually,
it looks like right over here,

03:17.680 --> 03:21.540
the slope of the tangent line
is it looks like the same

03:21.540 --> 03:23.110
as the slope of the secant line.

03:23.110 --> 03:25.030
It also looks like the
case right over here.

03:25.030 --> 03:27.340
The slope of the tangent
line is equal to the slope

03:27.340 --> 03:28.222
of the secant line.

03:28.222 --> 03:29.430
And it makes intuitive sense.

03:29.430 --> 03:31.740
At some point, your
instantaneous slope

03:31.740 --> 03:34.190
is going to be the same
as the average slope.

03:34.190 --> 03:37.210
Now how would we write
that mathematically?

03:37.210 --> 03:42.850
Well, let's calculate
the average slope

03:42.850 --> 03:44.080
over this interval.

03:44.080 --> 03:46.560
Well, the average slope
over this interval,

03:46.560 --> 03:49.220
or the average change, the
slope of the secant line,

03:49.220 --> 03:52.570
is going to be our change
in y-- our change in y

03:52.570 --> 03:55.785
right over here--
over our change in x.

04:00.530 --> 04:02.350
Well, what is our change in y?

04:02.350 --> 04:09.890
Our change in y is
f(b) minus f(a),

04:09.890 --> 04:14.910
and that's going to be
over our change in x.

04:14.910 --> 04:18.910
Over b minus b minus a.

04:18.910 --> 04:20.670
I'll do that in that red color.

04:20.670 --> 04:23.270
So let's just remind ourselves
what's going on here.

04:23.270 --> 04:25.870
So this right over here,
this is the graph of y

04:25.870 --> 04:27.360
is equal to f(x).

04:27.360 --> 04:29.770
We're saying that the
slope of the secant line,

04:29.770 --> 04:34.360
or our average rate of change
over the interval from a to b,

04:34.360 --> 04:42.720
is our change in y-- that the
Greek letter delta is just

04:42.720 --> 04:47.685
shorthand for change in
y-- over our change in x.

04:51.470 --> 04:53.580
Which, of course,
is equal to this.

04:53.580 --> 04:56.690
And the mean value
theorem tells us

04:56.690 --> 05:00.800
that there exists-- so
if we know these two

05:00.800 --> 05:04.030
things about the
function, then there

05:04.030 --> 05:12.700
exists some x value
in between a and b.

05:12.700 --> 05:17.596
So in the open interval between
a and b, there exists some c.

05:17.596 --> 05:19.490
There exists some
c, and we could

05:19.490 --> 05:23.702
say it's a member of the open
interval between a and b.

05:26.780 --> 05:33.860
Or we could say some c
such that a is less than c,

05:33.860 --> 05:35.590
which is less than b.

05:35.590 --> 05:37.696
So some c in this interval.

05:37.696 --> 05:43.210
So some c in between it
where the instantaneous rate

05:43.210 --> 05:47.320
of change at that
x value is the same

05:47.320 --> 05:49.110
as the average rate of change.

05:49.110 --> 05:52.290
So there exists some c
in this open interval

05:52.290 --> 05:55.500
where the average
rate of change is

05:55.500 --> 06:00.440
equal to the instantaneous
rate of change at that point.

06:00.440 --> 06:01.430
That's all it's saying.

06:01.430 --> 06:04.686
And as we saw this diagram right
over here, this could be our c.

06:04.686 --> 06:08.210
Or this could be our c as well.

06:08.210 --> 06:10.270
So nothing really--
it looks, you

06:10.270 --> 06:14.631
would say f is continuous over
a, b, differentiable over-- f

06:14.631 --> 06:16.880
is continuous over the closed
interval, differentiable

06:16.880 --> 06:18.750
over the open interval, and
you see all this notation.

06:18.750 --> 06:20.291
You're like, what
is that telling us?

06:20.291 --> 06:22.990
All it's saying is at some
point in the interval,

06:22.990 --> 06:25.220
the instantaneous
rate of change is

06:25.220 --> 06:28.430
going to be the same as
the average rate of change

06:28.430 --> 06:30.094
over the whole interval.

06:30.094 --> 06:31.760
In the next video,
we'll try to give you

06:31.760 --> 06:36.330
a kind of a real life example
about when that make sense.
